#515 / lcaconfbus1kill -- PRE-TABLE CHECK
Written BEFORE table 1 runs; its mtime is the order proof.

================================================================================
1. SIBLING PARITY -- the arms this diff touches vs their nearest siblings
================================================================================

The diff de-fatalises ONE arm (`else { fatal("TODO. Non-zero bus."); exit(1); }`) and adds one
refusal arm.  Its nearest siblings are the OTHER exits in the same file:

  lca_pci_conf   dev >= 21 scan miss   ALREADY de-fatalised upstream: the fatal()/exit(1) is
                                       COMMENTED OUT and `dev = 0' substituted.  This file's own
                                       graceful-degradation precedent -- and the arm under review
                                       was the outlier, not the policy.
  lca_isa        ofs > len             fatal()+exit(1), STILL LIVE.  Out of scope (separate row).
  lca_ioc        default read/write    fatal()+exit(1), STILL LIVE.  Out of scope (`lcabadval').
  lca_interrupt_assert / _deassert     bare fatal()+exit(1), NO GUARD.  Out of scope
                                       (`lcairqstubkill', separately witnessed).

ASYMMETRY FOUND, and it is DELIBERATE and disclosed rather than repaired here: the de-fatalised
scan-miss sibling substitutes a VALUE (dev = 0) and announces nothing.  This diff's refusal arm
does the same -- synthesises a value, announces nothing.  So the two arms are PARITY-MATCHED.
  -> no row owed for parity; the matching sibling is cited in the code comment.

A SECOND asymmetry IS live and is question Q2(b) to the panel: forwarding to an empty slot PRINTS
`[ bus_pci_data_access(): write to non-existant device ... ]', while the refusal is silent.  The
fix therefore makes the alias case QUIETER than the neighbouring empty-slot case.
  -> NOT a row.  It is a DISPOSITION question, put to the panel explicitly.

================================================================================
2. CLAIM -> ROW  (every added sentence asserting an OBSERVABLE CONSEQUENCE)
================================================================================

  "IOC_CONF bit 0 selects the cycle type only ... does NOT carry a bus number"
        -> ROW `t1 anchor': a type-1 write lands at the bus taken FROM THE ADDRESS (bus 1),
           not at the bus implied by ioc_conf.
  "a type-1 cycle carries its destination in the address"
        -> ROW `t1 anchor' (bus AND dev AND func all read back in the printed tuple).
  "a type-1 cycle whose bus field is zero selects NOTHING"
        -> ROW `t1 alias' (no forwarding diagnostic) AND ROW `t1 rdval' (the synthesised value).
  "Forwarding this case would ... hand the access to whichever primary-bus device matched"
        -> COUNTERFACTUAL-CONSEQUENCE claim.  It contains none of the five claim VERBS, and the
           verb list is NOT the test -- the test is semantic: does this sentence assert something
           a row could observe?  It does.  ROW `t1 alias' reds under M3_aliasoff, which IS that
           forwarding.
  "matching bus_pci_data_access's own empty-slot contract (all-ones at register 0, zero elsewhere)"
        -> ROW `t1 rdval' pins the all-ones half.  *** THE `zero elsewhere' HALF IS NOT ROWED. ***
           DISCLOSED, not silently skipped: the value 0 is also what an unmapped read returns, so
           such a row needs its own liveness control to be non-vacuous.  Coverage debt, clause (e).
  "Writes are discarded, which leaves backend state untouched"
        -> NOT ROWED and DISCLOSED.  `bus_pci_data_access' is never called on this arm, so
           "untouched" is true by construction rather than by observation; a row would test the
           absence of a call, which `t1 alias' already covers via the absent diagnostic.
  "MEASURED: two ordinary guest accesses, nothing refused anywhere on the path"
        -> the witness itself, 3/4 pre-fix; raws preserved beside this file.

================================================================================
3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
================================================================================

  guard reads:   type1 (= d->ioc_conf & 1),  bus (post-decode),  writeflag (sibling arm)
  body  reads:   bus, dev, func, reg, d->pci_data, writeflag, len, tag

DIFFERING FIELDS: `dev', `func', `reg', `tag' are read by the body and by neither guard.  Are they
written at the same sites as the guard's field?  NO -- and that is the interesting part:

  d->ioc_conf        written ONLY in lca_ioc's LCA_IOC_CONF case (one site)
  tag/dev/func/reg   derived from `relative_addr' INSIDE this function, on every call

So the guard's operand and the body's operands have ENTIRELY DIFFERENT PRODUCERS: one is sticky
device state set by an earlier store, the others are recomputed per access.  A state where they
DISAGREE is therefore not merely possible, it is the NORMAL case -- ioc_conf stays 1 while every
access carries a different address.
  -> ROW OWED and BUILT: `t1 anchor' (ioc_conf == 1, address selects bus 1) and `t1 alias'
     (ioc_conf == 1, address selects bus 0) are the two disagreeing states, and they take
     DIFFERENT arms.  That is the pair this check exists to demand.

