6. AMENDMENT after pass 2, BEFORE table 1.  Written as a DIFF against section 5,
   because the rule is that a row list rewritten from a panel's findings is DIFFED
   against this block's own prescribed rows, never merely extended with the new ones.
================================================================================

  CARRIED FORWARD FROM SECTION 5 -- none dropped:
    t1 rdval                                   ADDED, still present   (kills M2_nullval)
    reg != 0 half of the empty-slot contract   still NOT BUILT, still disclosed as clause-(e) debt
    type-1 forwarding to a REGISTERED device   still NOT BUILDABLE, still put to the panel as Q3

  ADDED BY THE PASS-2 PANEL:
    t0 write    ADDED.  *** A FLOOR CELL, prescribed by the deepseek seat. ***
                My Q1 enumeration listed the two type-1 READ cells as unrowed and MISSED that
                (type-0, WRITE) was unrowed as well.  It is inside the clause-(d) closure, not
                outside it: the guard's dominating predicates are `type1' and `bus == 0', and the
                guarded body TESTS `writeflag' twice in its own code, so the body-tested
                cross-product is {type-0, type-1} x {bus 0, bus != 0} x {read, write}.
                ESCAPE IT CLOSES, enumerated:
                    if ((type1 && bus == 0) || (!type1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE))
                leaves `t1 anchor' green (a type-1 WRITE still forwards and prints its tuple) and
                leaves `t0 accept' green (a type-0 READ still returns real config data).  Without
                this row it SWEEPS THE TABLE.

  RESOLVED AS UNROWABLE, with the derivation rather than a shrug:
    type-1, bus != 0, READ    NOT ROWABLE ON THIS MACHINE, and deepseek independently reached the
                same conclusion.  No device exists on any bus but 0, so this read is served by
                `bus_pci_data_access''s empty-slot arm and returns all-ones at register 0 --
                BYTE-IDENTICAL to what the refusal arm synthesises for the alias.  A value oracle
                therefore cannot separate them, and no other channel exists (reads print nothing;
                `bus_pci_setaddr' prints nothing).  Disclosed under clause (e).

  A CORRECTION TO THIS ROUND'S OWN RECORD, found by the minimax seat (see the round block):
    the addresses now carry the driver-side byte-enable bits `0x3 << 3'.  The decode is UNCHANGED
    -- re-derived independently: tag 0x11a00 / 0x00000 / 0x40000, and 0x10000 for the new row --
    because the handler discards bits 4..0 with `>> 5'.  What changes is that the probe now drives
    the address form a real driver emits, so the round's claim about what the printed tuple
    confirms is true rather than merely harmless.

  ROW COUNT: 10 at the time of section 5 -> 12 now.  BOTH ADDITIONS ARE L1 CONSTRUCTION
  (pre-ship), not reopenings of shipped material.

================================================================================
7. *** A WRONG CHANNEL LINE IN SECTION 4, FOUND BY THE grok SEAT ON PASS 2.
   THE CHANNEL CHECK CATCHING ITS OWN TABLE. Still before table 1. ***
================================================================================

SECTION 4 ASSERTED:  `t1 alias'  kills  M4_condwrite  via presence of `bus 0 func 0 device 0'.
THAT IS FALSE IN THE MIRROR POLARITY, and grok walked it rather than arguing it:

    if (type1 && bus == 0 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE)
        t1 alias   a WRITE at 0:0:0  -> still refused                        -> GREEN
        t1 rdval   a READ  at 0:0:0  -> forwarded to an EMPTY slot, all-ones -> GREEN
        t1 anchor  bus 1             -> untouched                            -> GREEN
    ==> CLEAN SWEEP OF THE ELEVEN-ROW PROBE.

My own M4 used the OPPOSITE polarity (`== MEM_READ'), which `t1 alias' does kill.  **So I had
covered the cell at exactly the address my own mutant used and left the symmetric cell open** --
the `#499' shape, reproduced inside the very table built to prevent it, by someone who had just
written the paragraph about it.

*** THE ROOT CAUSE IS THE ADDRESS, NOT THE POLARITY, AND THAT IS THE GENERAL LESSON HERE: EVERY
ALIAS ROW USED dev 0, AN EMPTY SLOT. ***  Forwarding to an empty slot returns the SAME all-ones the
refusal synthesises, so NO dev-0 row can EVER observe the misrouting -- for any mutant, in any
polarity.  The rows were blind by construction, not by bad luck.

**The fix's own comment names the right address and no row used it:** *"would ... hand the access to
whichever primary-bus device matched -- `i82378zb' is registered at 0:7:0"*.  At 0:7:0 the two
outcomes differ ABSOLUTELY: a refusal gives all-ones, a misroute gives the device's real config
data, 0x304848086.

  ROWS ADDED (both L1 construction, pre-ship):
    t1 dev7rd   type-1 READ  at bus 0 / dev 7 -- want 0xffffffffffffffff, ANTI-want the real data.
                ANCHORED in clause (f)'s sense: it names WHICH cell was reached, by a value only
                that one device can produce.  Kills M4 in the MEM_WRITE polarity.
    t1 dev7wr   type-1 WRITE at bus 0 / dev 7 -- want no `bus 0 func 0 device 7'.  Checkpointed on
                the following read's `print t2', so no-checkpoint renders differently from refused.
                Kills M4 in the MEM_READ polarity.

  SECTION 4's CHANNEL LINE IS SUPERSEDED FOR `t1 alias':
    t1 alias    kills M3_aliasoff ONLY.  It does NOT kill M4 in either polarity, and section 4's
                claim that it did is CORRECTED HERE rather than left to be read as true.
    t1 dev7rd/wr  kill M4 in both polarities.

  AND THE TABLE ITSELF GAINS THE MIRROR MUTANT: M4 is now run in BOTH polarities
  (M4a `== MEM_READ', M4b `== MEM_WRITE'), because running only the one my own repair anticipated
  is exactly what produced this defect.

  ROW COUNT: 10 -> 12 -> 14.

*** THE HONEST SCORE FOR THE CHANNEL RULE, since section 4 claimed its first mandated application
as a success: IT FOUND ONE FLOOR BREACH (t1 rdval) AND MISSED ANOTHER (this one). ***  The
difference between the two is instructive and is the part worth carrying: for `t1 rdval' I asked
"which mutant makes this row's observable differ?" and got a real answer.  For `t1 alias' I asked
the same question, ANSWERED IT FROM THE MUTANT I HAD ALREADY WRITTEN, and never asked whether a
DIFFERENT mutant of the same predicate would also be caught.  **The check is only as good as the
mutant set you bring to it, and an author's own mutant set is exactly the one shaped by the repair
the author just made.**  That is an argument for the ask-again and for the panel, not against the
rule -- but section 4's self-congratulation was premature and is withdrawn.

================================================================================
8. *** A THIRD CHANNEL FAILURE, AND THIS ONE THE TABLE CAUGHT RATHER THAN A SEAT.
   `t1 dev7wr' WAS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION -- the row added to close a floor
   breach could not fire under the mutant it was written for. ***
================================================================================

Section 7 added `t1 dev7wr' with its channel named as the EMPTY-SLOT write diagnostic,
`bus %i func %i device %i ]'.  That message comes from `bus_pci_data_access''s `dev == NULL' arm.

**0:7:0 IS THE ONE ADDRESS IN THIS DEVICE WHERE `dev' IS NOT NULL.**  It is the registered
i82378zb -- which is the entire reason section 7 chose it.  So the row's channel cannot fire at its
own address, under any mutant, ever.  **The row added to close a vacuity was itself vacuous, at the
address chosen to make the defect observable.**

WHAT A FORWARDED WRITE ACTUALLY PRINTS, from the M4a_condread run's evidence echo:

    [ bus_pci: write to PCI DATA: data = 0x00000001 (current value = 0x304848086);
      NOT YET SUPPORTED. bus 0, device 7, function 0 (i82378zb) register 0x00 ]

A REGISTERED device answers on a different path with a different message -- and that message is a
STRICTLY BETTER oracle than the one it replaces: it names the device BY NAME and quotes the config
data the write landed on.  Nothing but a real forward to the real i82378zb can produce it.
`D7W' is now `"device 7, function 0 (i82378zb)"'.

*** WHAT CAUGHT IT IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING, BECAUSE IT WAS NOT THE ROW AND NOT A SEAT. ***
Under M4a the row scored GREEN -- `refused' -- while the log directly above it printed the
misrouting in full.  **The probe's habit of ECHOING ITS EVIDENCE LINES INTO THE LOG is what made
the contradiction visible**, and that habit exists because of an earlier round where a green row's
evidence was tested in a pty buffer and never printed, leaving a preserved artifact that a later
reader could not check.

  A row said "refused".  Four lines above it, the log said the write reached the device.
  A contradiction is the cheapest signal there is, and it was only available because the
  evidence was echoed rather than merely tested.

*** THE HONEST TALLY FOR THE CHANNEL RULE ON ITS FIRST MANDATED APPLICATION IS NOW 1 FOUND, 2
MISSED. ***  It found `t1 rdval'.  It missed `t1 alias' vs the mirror mutant (grok), and it missed
`t1 dev7wr''s dead channel (the table).  Section 4 called the rule a success and section 7 withdrew
that; this withdraws it further.

**The pattern across all three is ONE mistake made three times: I named each channel by asking what
the code prints on the path I HAD IN MIND, never by asking what it prints on the path the MUTANT
takes.**  For `t1 alias' the mutant took the other polarity; for `t1 dev7wr' the mutant reached a
non-NULL device and a different printf entirely.

  > **THE CHANNEL LINE NEEDS A SECOND HALF: name the observable, then TRACE THE MUTANT'S OWN
  > CONTROL FLOW to the print it reaches.  A channel is only established when you can point at
  > the exact statement that emits it UNDER THE MUTANT -- not under the fix.**

That is a proposal about the carrier's own rule, so it is FILED for the flagship adjudication rather
than written into the carrier here -- three instances in one round is evidence, but a change to a
mandated check is an owner decision.
